Package: droidloom-runtime
Version: @DEB_VERSION@
Architecture: arm64
Maintainer: EvilBorsch <44337169+EvilBorsch@users.noreply.github.com>
Installed-Size: @INSTALLED_SIZE@
Depends: droidloom-image (>= @PKGVER@-@PKGREL@~), droidloom-image (<< @PKGVER@-@NEXT_PKGREL@~), libc6 (>= @LIBC_VERSION@), libgcc-s1, libgbm1, libxkbcommon0, libwayland-server0, libpipewire-0.3-0t64, libpam0g, libvulkan1, systemd (>= 254), pipewire, polkitd, pkexec, util-linux, mount, coreutils, iproute2, nftables, e2fsprogs, tar, zstd
Recommends: wireplumber, iptables, adb
Suggests: rsync
Section: misc
Priority: optional
Homepage: https://github.com/denialwm/droidloom
Description: Android apps in native Wayland windows (Droidloom runtime)
 Droidloom runs Android 17 (AOSP) userspace in Linux namespaces on the host
 kernel and shows every Android task as an ordinary Wayland window. This
 package holds the host side: the privileged cell daemon droidloomd, the
 Wayland presenter, the application catalog, audio, device and GPU brokers,
 droidloomctl, the setup helper and the systemd units.
 .
 The programs are built from commit @HOST_COMMIT@
 of https://github.com/EvilBorsch/droidloom, which is upstream release
 @PKGVER@-@PKGREL@ (commit @COMMIT@)
 with fixes for Debian and Ubuntu and these changes of behaviour: launching
 an application or installing an APK starts a stopped Droidloom first (an
 install sets it up first if it never was), and the launcher entries of
 Android's own applications are named "Settings (Android)". Android
 counts as ready once its user is unlocked, so every application opens
 right after a start. All other
 files are repacked unchanged from the upstream Arch Linux ARM package
 droidloom-runtime @PKGVER@-@PKGREL@, and two files are added: a udev
 rule that keeps Android's loop devices out of file managers
 (UDISKS_IGNORE), and a desktop entry that installs an opened APK file
 into Android and says in a notification which app it installed.
